You're going to get 2,

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:53.800
and then 20,

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:54.660
and then 200.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.080
But they're tending toward the same thing, right?

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.140
They're tending toward infinity.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:01.440
So,

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:02.360
That means

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.700
this is where this is going is

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.360
2 over 0.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.100
Right?

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.080
So apparently

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:09.520
1 over 0

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.240
is equal to infinity.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.260
And 2 over zero is equal to infinity.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:15.340
Right?

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.780
It's like well if you have

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:18.960
if you have these two things divided
by the same

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.640
they should be

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.560
they should be equal

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:22.640
Right?

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:23.920
Hmmm.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.300
Now, you see the reason why it was

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.500
so important to go establish this, right,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.960
is that you can see this is not just playing a game.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.400
We've broken, not just rules we decided or invented, right?

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.220
But something which we knew to be fundamentally wrong.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.400
Here's one!

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.600
I should put it in this hand, sorry.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.560
Here's one!

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.640
And here's two and they are not the same.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:48.140
Right?

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.120
They are two sets and they are different sizes.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.600
So this is, this is wrong, this is wrong.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:54.440
It can't be.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:55.100
Ok?

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.540
Which means that this can't be.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.280
Which means that none of that is true.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:05:59.740
Ok?

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:00.540
(therefore it's undefined?)

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.160
Now, ok, so...

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.040
Then comes the question:

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.120
Well then, ok,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.980
we call it, we call it, right?

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.420
Undefined.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.580
But why do we call it that?

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:12.900
Is it just because

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.820
It's like "Well I, I don't know."

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.380
And I'll show you why, I'll show you why.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.100
Let's come back to this, ok,

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.100
let's come back to this.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:23.140
Ok.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.760
Now, I approached it from this angle

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.140
and I was heading from this bigger number, 1

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.860
to .1, .01, and I was getting towards 0.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:31.700
That's the way I did it, right?

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:33.720
I approached it.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.800
Now, there's other ways to approach zero you know.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.380
You don't have to just come down from 1 towards 0.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.240
You could go, for instance,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.380
from -1

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.080
and go up towards 0.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.260
I can do that, can't I?

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.380
1 over this

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.180
1 over this.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.560
I'm still approaching 0.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.160
And I'm still going to get there.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.180
Minus .001

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:58.060
and so on.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.020
I should still get to 1 over 0.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.620
Ok, but what happens

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:02.860
Minus 1.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.020
Minus 10.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:06.760
Minus 100.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.480
Minus 1000?

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:10.780
Where's this going?

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.960
This is not going to infinity.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.820
It's going to negative infinity.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:18.560
Ok, now,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.720
This is where we get to the crux of
the issue, ok?

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:22.700
Undefined.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:24.580
Why do we call it undefined?

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:25.320
I used to think, y'know,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:26.580
you get your calculator out

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:27.680
and you punch it in

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.680
and it's like "math error" cause y'know

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.520
cause it's "undefined".

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:32.440
You look it up on Wikipedia.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.740
You're like "Are mathematicians just lazy?"

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.680
They're like "Ah, oh, I don't know what it is. We'll just not define it."

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:38.420
No.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:40.400
In fact

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.220
Undefined. It's undefined because

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:47.900
it's undefinable.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:48.960
Right?

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:50.940
There is no number you can pick

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:53.200
that makes sense, right?

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.700
Because you know you put this

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.120
and some other guy will say "But I can make it equal this

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.560
in exactly the same way you did."

00:07:58.980 --> 00:07:59.480
Right?

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.880
So there's equal sort of, um,

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.760
bidders for the crown

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:06.820
of what 1 over zero is equal to. Yes?

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.840
(it looks like if you graphed it as a hyperbola)

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:10.340
Umhm.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.320
(then it would never, cause it never touches zero)

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:13.820
Right.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.180
Ok, so, so,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.940
So this stuff here, right,

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.460
you can do it visually, right?

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.140
You've got this, right,

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:21.780
this is what we started with.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.960
We started at 1 over 1

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:23.940
which is like here.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:24.800
Right?

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:26.820
And then we decreased the value

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:27.780
of the denominator

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:28.880
1 over x, right?

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.840
So we were going this way

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.700
and off we go up to infinity.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.360
But you guys know what the
other side of the graph looks like, right?

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:39.420
That's the other side of 1 over x.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.800
And off it goes to negative infinity.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:42.540
Right?

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.400
So it's like, "Where am I going?"

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.800
Well the answer is, I can't be going anywhere.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:47.760
Right?

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:48.660
(because it doesn't exist)

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:49.920
That's right, that's right.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.540
The technical way of saying this is
that the limit does not exist.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.600
So, it's undefined because you can't define it.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.900
It's not just because we haven't worked out
what it's equal to yet.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.660
It's because we tried to work it out

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.040
and you just get completely random things, ok?

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.820
So that's why 1 over 0 is undefined

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.320
it can't be defined.

